messages:
mel: Opening a small PR to clarify recommended fallback behavior for transient 429s/timeouts. Need a concise pattern + tiny SDK snippets. PR: https://github.com/redwood-inference/docs/pull/1299
anita: Please define 'grace window' up front and emphasize opt-in. folks assume fallback is automatic otherwise.
chris: Ops wants a 3-step troubleshooting checklist (synthetic repro, kv cache check, model-selection logs). Keep it short.
sdk-bot: SDK guidance: show try/except (py) and async/await (js). we don't yet expose a fallback_model param in stable SDKs. Example py snippet:
```python
try:
  resp = client.generate(model="titan-large", input=text)
except TooManyRequestsError:
  time.sleep(backoff())
  resp = client.generate(model="titan-small", input=text)
```
and JS:```js
try {
  const r = await client.generate({model: 'titan-large', input})
} catch (e) {
  await sleep(backoff())
  const r2 = await client.generate({model: 'titan-small', input})
}
```
mel: add note about cache implications: fallback may lose KV/prefix cache warmness, affecting latency. also a "Known limitations" bullet about streaming not preserved when switching mid-request.
anita: I'll confirm billing: we need exact wording on whether tokens are billed at attempted model vs final model. will update PR comment.
deploy-bot: PR #1299 created by mel. CI: doc-lint OK, link-check pending, preview deployed at 2026-03-22T20:04:00Z.
chris: one-liner FAQ: "Should I enable fallback?" Answer: "Opt-in; use when latency SLOs matter more than top-end quality; test with your prompts"
mel: merging after billing confirms. thanks all :thumbsup:
